<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Arretium</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 294 BCE (approx.) · capital Arretium</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Northern Etruscan city, home of the bronze Chimera of Arezzo, which grew rich on farming and metalworking and became an early Roman ally.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Arretium, Etruscan Aritim, modern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-arezzo">Arezzo</a>, lay where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/val-di-chiana">Val di Chiana</a> meets the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arno">Arno</a>, controlling routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a> and the Po valley. It developed into a city from the 6th century BCE, later than most Etruscan centres, and grew powerful in the 4th century BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Arretium was known for agriculture and metalwork. The bronze <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chimera-of-arezzo">Chimera of Arezzo</a>, cast around 400 BCE and found in 1553, is a masterpiece of Etruscan art. The city was dominated by great families, among them the Cilnii, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> reports being driven out by a popular revolt in 302 BCE and restored with Roman help; the Augustan minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-maecenas">Maecenas</a> was descended from them.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Arretium made truces with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 310 and 294 BCE and became a loyal ally, supplying weapons and grain to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio</a> in 205 BCE. Under Rome it became famous for Arretine ware, a red-glazed tableware exported across the empire. The town continued as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-arezzo">Arezzo</a>, birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/arretium.jpg" alt="Arretium" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Andrewrabbott · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piazza_Grande,_Arezzo.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 294 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Arretium</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Etruscan religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Etruscan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Arezzo (Etruscan), Aritim</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-547&amp;focus=arretium" aria-label="Arretium on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4966 636 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Arretium lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5286 892L5290 918M5286 892L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5290 918h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5286 892h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5286 892h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-547&amp;focus=arretium">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

